(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-cocci-of-medical-importance-quiz"]={"title":"Cocci of Medical Importance — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Gram-positive cells are described as physically strong. How are Gram-negative cells described?","choices":["Physically strong","Heat resistant","Spore protected","Chemically strong"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Gram-positive cells are physically strong from thick peptidoglycan, whereas Gram-negative cells are chemically strong. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which enzyme, produced by 97% of human isolates, is a significant diagnostic trait of Staphylococcus aureus?","choices":["Coagulase","Staphylokinase","Hyaluronidase","Lipase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: coagulase coagulates plasma and blood, and its presence is what separates Staphylococcus aureus from the coagulase-negative staphylococci. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which Staphylococcus aureus toxin lyses neutrophils and macrophages?","choices":["Coagulase","Leukocidin","Enterotoxin","Exfoliative toxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: leukocidin lyses leukocytes, especially neutrophils and macrophages, the cells sent to attack the organism. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does toxic shock syndrome toxin induce?","choices":["Separation of the epidermis","Lysis of neutrophils","Digestion of blood clots","Fever, vomiting and shock"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the toxin induces fever, vomiting, shock and systemic organ damage, a toxemia that can end in organ failure. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which staphylococcal lesion is a boil, in which a hair follicle or sebaceous gland progresses into an abscess?","choices":["Furuncle","Carbuncle","Folliculitis","Impetigo"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a furuncle is a boil, inflammation of a hair follicle or sebaceous gland that progresses into an abscess or pustule. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where does Staphylococcus capitis live?","choices":["Scalp, face and external ear","Armpits and groin","Gums and teeth","Vagina and intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Staphylococcus capitis lives on the scalp, face and external ear. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where does Staphylococcus epidermidis normally live?","choices":["Skin and mucous membranes","Scalp, face and external ear","Armpit and groin sweat glands","Human large intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it lives on skin and mucous membranes, and can cause wound infection by penetrating broken skin. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which staphylococcus infrequently lives on skin, intestine and vagina and may cause urinary tract infection?","choices":["Staphylococcus hominis","Staphylococcus aureus","Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Staphylococcus capitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Staphylococcus saprophyticus infrequently lives on skin, intestine and vagina and may cause urinary tract infection. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where does Staphylococcus hominis live?","choices":["On the scalp and external ear","In the anterior nares","In the vagina and intestine","Around apocrine sweat glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it lives around the apocrine sweat glands of the armpits and groin, which produce sweat with a higher lipid content. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Staphylococcus aureus grows on a selective medium on which streptococci grow poorly. Which property lets it grow there?","choices":["It is catalase negative","It needs extra carbon dioxide","It tolerates high salinity","It needs blood in the medium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Staphylococcus aureus withstands high salinity, so it grows on mannitol salts agar, which is too saline for the streptococci. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How do streptococci react in the catalase test?","choices":["Oxidase positive","Catalase negative","Catalase positive","Coagulase positive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: streptococci do not produce catalase, though they do have a peroxidase system; staphylococci are catalase positive. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which of these is alpha hemolytic?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Streptococcus pyogenes","Streptococcus agalactiae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Streptococcus pneumoniae and the viridans group are alpha hemolytic, producing partial, greenish hemolysis. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A beta-hemolytic streptococcus is bacitracin sensitive. What is it most likely to be?","choices":["Streptococcus pyogenes","Streptococcus agalactiae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Streptococcus mutans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: bacitracin-sensitive beta-hemolytic streptococci are group A, Streptococcus pyogenes. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which streptococcus is strictly a human parasite, inhabiting the throat, nasopharynx and occasionally the skin?","choices":["Enterococcus faecalis","Streptococcus mutans","Streptococcus pyogenes","Streptococcus agalactiae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: group A Streptococcus pyogenes is strictly a human parasite, and humans are its only reservoir. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does M-protein contribute to Streptococcus pyogenes?","choices":["Resistance to phagocytosis","Protection from lysozyme","Adherence by short fibers","Blocking of complement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: M-protein contributes to its resistance to phagocytosis. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which group A streptococcal sequela causes nephritis and raised blood pressure and can lead to kidney failure?","choices":["Urinary tract infection","Acute glomerulonephritis","Rheumatic fever","Toxic shock syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: acute glomerulonephritis causes nephritis and increased blood pressure, and can become chronic, leading to kidney failure. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does group B Streptococcus reach the newborn?","choices":["By contaminated food","Through a tooth socket","During vaginal delivery","Across the placenta"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it can be transferred to the infant during vaginal delivery and cause severe infection in the child. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A pregnant woman is expected to deliver vaginally. What is recommended for group B Streptococcus?","choices":["Plan a routine cesarean","Give lifelong penicillin","Screen her and treat if positive","Vaccinate the infant at birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: pregnant women should be screened and treated, usually with intravenous antibiotics during vaginal delivery. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which infections do the enterococci cause, particularly in debilitated persons?","choices":["Meningitis and septicemia","Pharyngitis and scarlet fever","Caries and periodontitis","Urinary, wound and skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: enterococci cause opportunistic urinary, wound and skin infections, particularly in debilitated persons. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 62-year-old with pre-existing heart valve disease develops endocarditis weeks after a tooth extraction. Which group of organisms is most likely?","choices":["Group B streptococci","Gram-negative diplococci","Viridans streptococci","Group A streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a dental procedure lets these oral residents into the blood, and pre-existing heart disease puts the patient at high risk of subacute endocarditis. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What varies among the capsular types of Streptococcus pneumoniae?","choices":["Its hemolysis pattern","Its specific soluble substance","Its Lancefield group letter","Its Gram stain reaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the specific soluble substance of the capsule varies among types, which is why there are many different capsular types. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Pneumococcal infections are usually endogenous. What does that mean?","choices":["From another patient's blood","From the person's own flora","From contaminated surfaces","From an animal reservoir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: 5 to 50% of people carry it in the nasopharynx, and infection usually comes from that carriage, especially if the host is immunocompromised. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does a candle jar provide when culturing pathogenic Neisseria on chocolate agar?","choices":["Extra salt in the medium","Complete absence of air","Red cells for hemolysis","Enhanced carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: pathogenic species need carbon dioxide-enhanced chambers, such as chocolate agar in a candle jar. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How is gonococcal conjunctivitis of the newborn prevented?","choices":["A conjugate vaccine at birth","Long-term oral penicillin","Topical antibiotics at birth","Routine cesarean delivery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is prevented by topical antibiotic prophylaxis immediately after birth. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is the public health status of gonorrhea?","choices":["A vaccine-preventable one","A reportable disease","A zoonotic disease","A foodborne disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: gonorrhea is a reportable infectious disease, and it ranks in the top 5 sexually transmitted diseases. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What gives a presumptive identification of gonorrhea from urethral, vaginal, cervical or eye exudate?","choices":["Clusters of cocci in pus","Diplococci inside neutrophils","Lancet pairs in sputum","Chains of cocci in pus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Gram-negative intracellular diplococci within neutrophils are a presumptive identification. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How does Neisseria meningitidis reach the meninges from the nasopharynx?","choices":["Through a skin wound","Through the bloodstream","Through the eustachian tube","Along peripheral nerves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: disease begins when the bacteria enter the bloodstream, cross the blood-brain barrier, permeate the meninges and grow in the cerebrospinal fluid. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which setting raises the risk of meningococcal disease in young adults?","choices":["Military field hospitals","Dental surgery suites","Tampon use in women","College dormitories"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: carriage is higher in institutional settings, and those living in close quarters, such as college dorms, are at high risk. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Where is Branhamella (Moraxella) catarrhalis found in the body?","choices":["The large intestine","The apocrine glands","The heart valves","The nasopharynx"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it is found in the nasopharynx, and the diplococci live on mucous membranes of humans and other primates. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 26-year-old soldier evacuated from Afghanistan develops a multidrug-resistant infection with coccobacilli in a military hospital. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Acinetobacter baumannii","Moraxella catarrhalis","Neisseria meningitidis","Enterococcus faecalis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: these multidrug-resistant coccobacilli are becoming an important hospital-acquired infection, especially in military hospitals. — PAJ5200.Cocci of Med importance.pptx, Slide 79"}]};
